[Music]  hi it's Michele from lab muffin beauty  science here chemistry PhD and big fan  of sunscreen  summer is coming for those of you in the  northern hemisphere and that means the  Sun is coming out and with it all of the  sunscreen myths ever so today I'm going  to go through the top sunscreen myths in  my opinion if I've missed your favorite  or least favorite myth leave me a  comment so I know to include it next  time if you want to know more about how  to choose and use the sunscreen that's  right for your skin you can check out my  ebook the lab muffin guide to basic skin  care how does the sunscreen chapter is  available as a sample on my site check  it out with the link below and of course  if you like this sort of video click the  thumbs up subscribe and click the  notification bellow  let's start with the first myth SPF 50  is only a little bit better than SPF 30  this myth turns up a lot and it goes  something like this  SPF 15 blocks 93% of UV SPF 30 blocks  97% of UV SPF 50 blocks 98% of UV and  since SPF 50 only blocks 1% more UV than  SPF 30 it isn't worth using SPF 50 for  the tiny amount of extra protection this  is one of the most persistent myths even  amongst dermatologists it's so  persistent that a group of sunscreen  scientists wrote a letter published in  JM a dermatology same media and health  professionals often incorrectly state  that SPS beyond 30 of the only minor  improvements in sun protection I talked  about this myth before in my video on  the meaning of SPF the reason for this  myth is that it looks at the amount of  UV block rather than the amount of UV  that the sunscreen allows into your skin  SPF 30 allows one and a half times as  much UV into your skin compared to SPF  50 it's actually more accurate to say  that it's a hundred and fifty percent as  effective or fifty percent more  effective you'll get burnt one and a  half times as fast with SPF 30 for  example this bears out in practice as  well with multiple studies showing that  the amount of protection scales with the  SPF number there's a study where people  applied SPF 100 plus sunscreen on one  side of their face and SPF 50 plus on  the other side after six hours of  activity in the Sun more than half the  people were more sunburned on the  spf50 side while only 5% of people were  more sunburned on the SPF 100 side if we  do the math according to the myth SPF  100 will only be 1% more effective so  this result makes no sense but according  to the way we should be measuring it SPF  100 plus is twice as effective which  explains the results way better so what  we should be doing instead according to  the science is to just use the SPF  number as a guide to the amount of  protection we get when we actually use  the sunscreen on our skin in other words  SPF correlates with clinical  effectiveness you can make an effective  DIY sunscreen from zinc oxide powder or  diaper cream no don't do this it might  save some money in the short term but do  you know what costs even more skin  cancer I have a whole video on the  reasons why giy sunscreen is a bad idea  but basically you can't make sure that  you've made a product that's going to  keep the zinc oxide spread evenly on  your skin at home it's hard enough for  cosmetic chemists to do it with  industrial machines and prepare emulsion  systems and proper SPF testing so the  chance that you can do it at home with a  jar of zinc powder or diaper cream is  just minuscule you shouldn't play lotto  with your health like that wearing  sunscreen is worse for you than not  wearing sunscreen there are a few  reasons that people give to justify this  myth firstly that sunscreen filters are  bad for you because they can disrupt  your hormones and nanoparticles can  absorb into your body leading to various  diseases there are a few studies that  have found that some sunscreen filters  have potential hormonal effects the  problem is that most of these studies  were done on cell lines and animals so  they don't necessarily translate to  human effects at the amount used in  sunscreens results from animal studies  have been inconsistent and harmful  effects were only seen when very large  amounts of sunscreen chemicals were fed  to the animals one paper did actually  try to translate it to what happens in a  human and it found that the sunscreen  with the strongest hormonal effects  which is oxybenzone if you applied a  sunscreen with a large amount of it  you'd have to use it continuously for 35  years over a hundred percent of your  body to have a noticeable hormonal  effect there are a few studies on  whether sunscreen chemicals have  hormonal effects in humans  but the results have been mixed some  observational studies have found that  people with higher oxybenzone levels  tended to have some reproductive  characteristics but not others a trial  where and oxybenzone containing cream  was applied didn't have any effects on  reproductive hormones so while it's  possible that there's a hormonal effect  it's unlikely to be significant  according to what we currently know  there are also nanoparticles in  sunscreens zinc oxide and titanium  dioxide the inorganic physical  sunscreens are ground up into tiny  nanometer sized particles in lots of  sunscreens and this improves their sun  protection properties and stops them  from looking white on your skin it's  possible that they can absorb into your  body and have an effect but the studies  so far show that they don't make a past  the top dead layers of your skin the  stratum corneum unless you apply it to  broken skin the second reason for this  myth is that sun exposure has been  linked to better health the problem with  this is that it doesn't show that sun  exposure with sunscreen doesn't have the  same benefits there are a few scientific  studies that have found that people who  get more sun exposure tend to be  healthier one big issue with these  studies first off is that a lot of the  time it doesn't tell you the reasons why  people are or aren't getting sun  exposure if someone is sick with a  chronic illness or a terminal disease  they're not going to get out as much and  so they're going to get less Sun  exposure people who exercise more will  be healthier and live longer and as a  side-effect they end up getting more  sunlight so correlation doesn't  necessarily mean causation there are a  few studies that have controlled for  some of these variables and more sun  exposure still lead to better health but  this still doesn't mean that wearing the  sunscreen while you're getting the sun  exposure is a bad idea  the proposed reasons for why sun  exposure is good for us is mostly to do  with vitamin D and nitric oxide  production vitamin D is made in your  skin when you're exposed to UVB light  while nitric oxide is produced in your  body in response to UVA vitamin D is  good for bone health and there's also  evidence that it could be involved in  preventing cancer heart disease  metabolic syndrome and obesity nitric  oxide is good for cardiovascular or  heart health  there haven't been any Studies on nitric  oxide in sunscreen use but there's been  a couple of studies on vitamin D in  sunscreen since sunscreen reduces the  amount of UV  be that reaches your skin and causes you  to get a sunburn it seems logical that  sunscreen would decrease vitamin D  production but studies have found that  it actually has very little effect in  real life the reason seems to be that  most people don't apply sunscreen  sufficiently and even if you think you  do there are lots of spots that you'll  have to miss things like your scalp  between your fingers and so on and so  these little gaps are enough to get you  the UV for adequate vitamin D levels  there's a lot less research on nitric  oxide but since it's also UV on the skin  that causes the nitric oxide production  the best guess is that it will probably  be a similar story even if you use  sunscreen  you'll probably get enough sunlight for  nitric oxide there are a lot of benefits  to wearing sunscreen the main months are  obviously skin cancer prevention and  prevention of skin aging in Australia  skin cancer is the most common type of  cancer it represents over 80% of new  cancer cases every year melanoma which  is a particularly deadly form of skin  cancer is the fourth most common cancer  just by itself in 2018 there were 14 and  a half thousand new melanoma diagnosis  and 1,900 deaths from melanoma the  number of melanoma jeff's adjusted for  population size has been increasing as  well in 2016 melanoma skin cancer was  the 12th leading cause of cancer death  in Australia and it's estimated that it  was the eighth most common in 2018 about  10% of people diagnosed with melanoma  will die within five years there've been  Studies on the benefits of sunscreen use  and it's been found that it decreases  skin cancer and skin aging in the  biggest study on this so far conducted  in Australia daily sunscreen use led to  decreased signs of aging and invasive  melanoma sware about four times less  common another study estimated that the  melanoma rate in Norway in older women  could drop by 18 percent with the daily  use of an SPF 15 plus sunscreen the  recommendation in Australia is for  sensible sun exposure combined with  sunscreen based on the UV level if the  UV level is 3 or above in the middle of  the day you should be wearing sunscreen  but if not then it's recommended that  you should intentionally get some sun  exposure there's more information about  this in my video on vitamin D and  sun protection chemical sunscreen only  works if you apply it to bare skin  there's a myth that chemical or organic  sunscreens need to absorb into your skin  and bind before they start working but  actually all sunscreen ingredients both  chemical and physical or more correctly  organic and inorganic work mostly the  same way they form a film that can  absorb UV light and turn it into heat a  few ingredients like zinc oxide and  titanium dioxide and Tino Saab M will  also reflect and scatter a small amount  of UV but all sunscreen ingredients will  mostly be converting UV into heat energy  and all of these sunscreen ingredients  will work before you apply them on your  skin you should still be applying them  around 20 minutes before sun exposure  because the sunscreen product itself  needs to spread out and dry down and  form an even layer on your skin before  will be protecting your skin effectively  but this is for all sunscreens and not  just the chemical sunscreens makeup with  SPF gives you enough protection it's  tempting to see an SPF rating on your  foundation or BB cream and think that  it's enough to protect you but the  amount of makeup that you use is way too  tiny you need two milligrams of product  per square centimeter of skin to get the  SPF rating on the label if you use less  the SPF protection will decrease a whole  bunch as well I did the maths in my  video on sunscreen and makeup questions  you need around one point to your  grounds of product on your face to get  to milligrams per square centimeter with  my regular use I'd get maybe SPF true  from an SPF 50 powder and SPF 8 from an  SPF 50 foundation the other issue is  that makeup usually doesn't have  broad-spectrum protection so you won't  be protecting your skin adequately from  UVA rays especially the longer  wavelengths that don't need to be  blocked to get a high SPF rating  layering products with SPF doesn't  increase the SPF this myth says that  there's no benefit to layering multiple  SPF rated products because the total SPF  you'll get is the SPF of the product  with the highest SPF so if you wear a  moisturizer with SPF 15 and a sunscreen  with SPF 30 and a foundation with SPF 5  the highest you can get is SPF 30 this  is sort of true  true if you're applying two milligrams  per square centimeter of sunscreening  product and that's the maximum amount of  product you can fit on your face because  any extra product that you add will just  wipe off but almost no one applies this  much and even if you measure out your  sunscreen properly you probably aren't  applying it perfectly evenly so the  extra layers of SPF will give you more  protection or cover up for any  insufficient parts there's a study that  found that applying two layers of  sunscreen was effective at increasing  sun protection the subjects ended up  with about two and a half times more  protection but if you're layering  sunscreen there's also the possibility  that different sunscreen ingredients can  destabilize each other leading to less  protection so make sure you check that  there aren't any incompatibilities first  it's safest to stick to sunscreens with  similar active ingredients you don't  need to reapply sunscreen if you haven't  been in the Sun there's a myth that the  only reason you need to reapply  sunscreen is because the chemical  filters and them break down after they  absorb too much UV but this isn't the  only reason they can be rubbed off as  you there on your skin and less  sunscreen on your skin means more UV  gets through the sunscreen film also  breaks up and gets thinner over time a  couple of the older chemical or organic  sunscreen filters were also photo  unstable and this means they would break  down after too much UV exposure but a  lot of chemical filters Arthur to stable  and so they won't have this problem you  can think of it like a coat of paint a  photo unstable UV filter is like a paint  that changes color or gets bleached by  the Sun you need to repaint it if that  happens but you also need to repaint it  if the paint just chips off sunscreen  comes off a lot faster if you're being  active in the Sun but I'll also wear off  if you move around a lot in the shade  there's evidence that applying it once  daily still gives lots of benefits but  if you need higher sun protection or if  you're being very active then  reapplication is definitely a good idea  regardless of the amount of sun there  are a lot of other sunscreen myths too  like whether it's destroying coral reefs  and whether chemical or physical  sunscreens are better I've covered a lot  of these in my other videos on sunscreen  so I recommend checking them out if you  want to find out more if you're  interested in finding out how to choose  a sunscreen that's right for your skin  and how to use them most effectively  check out the loud muffin guide to basic  skincare leave me a comment with the  myths that I miss that you think are  important and again if you liked this  video give it a thumbs up and subscribe  to my channel for more Beauty size  videos you can also check out my blog  and Instagram see you next time for more  beauty science

